leak_test_details:
I wrote my own test, /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/83a4d5f8-8137-5cb1-9baa-8ac55fed2a66/scratchpad/ml/verify_minutes/leak.js. It covered rounds 5, 12, 20 and 33 of 2022-23, 2023-24 and 2024-25 (12 cases). For each case it builds a truncated copy of the season:
- history rows with round >= r are removed;
- fixtures of round >= r are marked unfinished, with no score;
- every numeric player column after the first 8 is nulled, except 'code';
- events are rewritten so that round r is the next event;
- scoring_gw is also dropped.
Round r's history rows are not in the file. To define the target players and the club they played for in round r, the test uses separate stats-free stubs that carry only opp, h and fx, with no points or minutes.

Two code paths were checked against the full-data btRun replay for round r:
(A) btRound on the truncated data;
(B) production pmPredictAll on the truncated data, with player totals rebuilt from the truncated history and status 'a'.

All 12 cases passed. There were 0 missing and 0 extra players, and the maximum absolute difference was 0.0 for pred, and for pp and eMin in path A. No future rows remained.

Positive control: I injected a small leak that reads FPD end-of-season total_points into pPlay0 (in /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/83a4d5f8-8137-5cb1-9baa-8ac55fed2a66/scratchpad/ml/verify_minutes/leaky_control.js). The test flagged it as FAIL, with pred differences of about 4e-3, so it does detect leaks.

The code also shows why the change is leak-free by construction. It only reads `rows`, which is the player's hist with round < r inside btRound, and it does not touch totals, prices, xP or other seasons.
